From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Reed <reedswork@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 15:17:45 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] client: say when a request's connection ended before any
 byte of its response

A server that closes an idle keep-alive connection as a request reaches
it answers nothing, and the client cannot tell that from a server that
read the request and then failed: both end as "connection closed before
message completed" (IncompleteMessage), and so does a response whose head
was cut off. Chrome sends such a request again when the connection was a
kept one and no byte of a response came (HttpNetworkTransaction's
ShouldResendRequest), whatever the method, so a client needs to know which
it was.

`Error::is_empty_response()` says the request's connection ended before
any byte of its response arrived. HTTP/1: the connection counts the bytes
it reads, and an error handed to the request's callback is marked when
none came since its head was written (an informational response counts;
a cut-off head or body does not qualify). HTTP/2: a stream that failed
before its response head because its connection went away (the server's
GOAWAY, or the connection closed or failed); a reset stream does not
qualify.

`Error::is_refused_stream()` says the server reset the HTTP/2 stream with
REFUSED_STREAM, which RFC 9113 8.7 says it did not process; Chrome sends
such a request again from any connection.
